# Line endings, pinned.
#
# This repo had no .gitattributes and ran on `core.autocrlf=true`, so every
# commit printed "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" —
# the tools here write LF, and git wanted CRLF back in the working tree. A
# warning that fires on every commit is a warning nobody reads, and this repo
# leans on byte-comparisons: a line-ending policy that depends on one machine's
# git config is a silent way for two sessions to disagree about a file's bytes.
#
# Pinned to LF on BOTH sides — index and working tree. Measured before pinning:
# the working tree was already 214 of 215 markdown and 148 of 148 python files
# LF, so this ratifies what the record already is rather than rewriting it. The
# 17 stragglers (15 json, docs/experiments/README.md, .gitignore) were converted
# in this commit, and the index DB was rebuilt across the conversion to check
# the parse is not line-ending sensitive: byte-identical, 6b879d3c…, so nothing
# derived moves.
#
# Order matters here: later rules win, so the binary marks must follow the
# catch-all.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.ps1   text eol=lf

# Binary — never touched by the eol filter, never diffed as text. `.db` is the
# derived index, `.npy`/`.glb` are not tracked today but are the route's own
# artifact types and will be.
*.db    binary
*.png   binary
*.npy   binary
*.glb   binary
